## Buying a refurbished MacBook

MacBooks hold their value well and are built to last, which makes them a natural fit for refurbishment. A well-refurbished MacBook gives you the full macOS experience — excellent build quality, a great trackpad and sharp display — at a significantly lower price than buying new.

### Apple silicon or Intel?

Apple moved its Macs from Intel processors to its own **Apple silicon** chips (M1, M2, M3 and later) starting in late 2020. The difference is significant:

- **Apple silicon MacBooks** are faster, run cooler and quieter, and have much longer battery life. They are also expected to receive macOS updates for longer.
- **Intel MacBooks** can be more affordable and support running Windows natively through Boot Camp, which some specialist software needs.

For most buyers, an **M1 MacBook Air or newer** is the best-value choice.

### MacBook Air vs MacBook Pro

- **MacBook Air** — light, fanless on Apple silicon models, and ideal for study, writing, browsing and office work.
- **MacBook Pro** — adds more performance headroom, better cooling and, on many models, brighter displays and more ports. Better for sustained video editing, music production and development work.

### What we check on every MacBook

- **Activation Lock and accounts** — every MacBook is checked to be free of the previous owner's Apple ID.
- **Battery** — cycle count and condition are measured. Apple publishes a rated maximum cycle count for each model (1,000 for most recent MacBooks).
- **Keyboard, trackpad, display and ports** — tested as part of our [refurbishment process](/how-we-refurbish).

You can also check a MacBook's battery yourself — see [how to check battery health](/blog/how-to-check-laptop-battery-health). Before buying, make sure any software you need runs on macOS. Compare grades on our [condition grades](/condition-grades) page.
